= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $N$ be the zero-starting <continuous local martingale>, and let $V_t$ be the total variation of its path on $[0,t]$. Stop when $|N|$ or $V$ reaches $n$, and also at time $n$. The stopped process is bounded, hence a true <square-integrable> <martingale> by the <bounded local martingale criterion>, and its total variation is bounded by $n$.

For a deterministic partition $0=t_0<\cdots<t_m=t$, <martingale> increments are orthogonal in $L^2$, so
$$
\mathbb E(N_{t\wedge\tau_n}^2)=\sum_j\mathbb E(N_{t_j\wedge\tau_n}-N_{t_{j-1}\wedge\tau_n})^2.
$$
The sum inside the <expectation> is at most the largest absolute increment times the stopped total variation. As the mesh tends to zero, path continuity makes the largest increment tend to zero. The product is bounded by $2n^2$, so the <dominated convergence theorem> makes the displayed <expectation> zero. Thus the stopped process vanishes at every fixed time. Use rational times, continuity and $\tau_n\uparrow\infty$ to conclude
$$
\boxed{N_t=0\text{ for all }t\ge0\text{ outside one null set}.}
$$
This proves that a <continuous finite-variation local martingale is constant>.

Write a normalized <semimartingale decomposition> as $X=X_0+N+A$, with both $N_0=A_0=0$, $N$ a <continuous local martingale> and $A$ of <finite variation>. The difference of two such <martingale> parts is also the difference of two finite-variation parts, and hence vanishes by the result just proved. The other parts coincide too. **The normalized <continuous semimartingale> decomposition is unique.** Fixing the initial constants is necessary: without normalization a constant can be transferred between the two parts.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Fix a sequence of deterministic partitions with mesh tending to zero and define the completed-increment sums
$$
S_t^{(n)}=\sum_j\bigl(X_{t\wedge t_{j+1}^{(n)}}-X_{t\wedge t_j^{(n)}}\bigr)^2.
$$
For a <continuous semimartingale>, these sums converge to $[X]_t$ in <uniform convergence on compacts in probability>, the standard path-increment formula for <quadratic variation>. The sums use only the path of $X$, and do not mention a <filtration>. If the same process is a <semimartingale> for two <filtrations>, the same sums have both proposed <quadratic variations> as limits. Uniqueness of limits in probability, followed by continuity, makes these limits indistinguishable.

For the change of measure, let $R=d\mathbb Q/d\mathbb P$. If $\mathbb P(A_n)\to0$, then
$$
\mathbb Q(A_n)=\mathbb E_{\mathbb P}(R\mathbf1_{A_n})\longrightarrow0:
$$
truncate $R$ at a large constant, use $\mathbb E[R\mathbf1_{\{R>K\}}]\to0$, and then let $\mathbb P(A_n)\to0$. Apply this argument to the event $\sup_{s\le t}|S_s^{(n)}-[X]_s^{\mathbb P}|>\varepsilon$. The same sums converge to the P-version of the bracket in Q-probability, and the Q-semimartingale formula makes them converge to the Q-version. Therefore
$$
\boxed{[X]^{\mathbb Q}=[X]^{\mathbb P}\quad\mathbb Q\text{-indistinguishably}.}
$$
This is <quadratic variation under an absolutely continuous measure change>. With only $\mathbb Q\ll\mathbb P$, the equality is asserted under Q: a Q-version is unconstrained on a Q-null set that might have positive P-probability.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For each fixed horizon $t$, the stipulated property is the <Class D process> condition on $[0,t]$, and imposing it for every finite horizon gives a <Class DL process>.

First suppose $M$ is a true <martingale>. For every <stopping time> $T\le t$, the bounded-time <optional sampling theorem> gives
$$
M_T=\mathbb E(M_t\mid\mathcal F_T).
$$
The <conditional expectations> of one integrable variable form a <uniformly integrable> family. To see the uniformity explicitly, put $V=\mathbb E(U\mid\mathcal H)$. For any $r,K>0$, conditional Jensen and Markov inequalities give
$$
\mathbb E\bigl[|V|\mathbf1_{\{|V|>K\}}\bigr]
\le\mathbb E\bigl[|U|\mathbf1_{\{|U|>r\}}\bigr]+\frac{r\mathbb E|U|}{K}.
$$
Choose $r$ large and then $K$ large. The bound does not depend on the conditioning <sigma-algebra>. This proves the required <uniform integrability>. Bounded-time optional sampling here can also be justified directly: approximate $T$ from above by finite-grid <stopping times>, use discrete optional sampling with terminal value $M_t$, and use this same conditional-expectation <uniform integrability> and right continuity to pass to the limit.

Conversely, take a <localizing sequence> $\tau_n$ for the <local martingale>. For fixed $s\le t$, both families $M_{t\wedge\tau_n}$ and $M_{s\wedge\tau_n}$ are <uniformly integrable>, because their times are bounded by $t$. They converge almost surely to $M_t$ and $M_s$, and therefore converge in $L^1$. The stopped <martingale> identity
$$
\mathbb E(M_{t\wedge\tau_n}\mid\mathcal F_s)=M_{s\wedge\tau_n}
$$
passes to the limit by $L^1$ contractivity of <conditional expectation>. Thus $\mathbb E(M_t\mid\mathcal F_s)=M_s$, and all time values are integrable. Hence **a <local martingale> is a true <martingale> exactly when it is of <class DL>**. This is a finite-horizon criterion, not a claim of <uniform integrability> over all times simultaneously.

